"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/158332"} "36 from 18 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "There was no particularly strong favorite for the coxless pairs event at the 1960 Summer Olympics: none of the most recent victors from the European Championships, British Empire and Commonwealth Games, or the Silver Goblets &amp; Nickalls’ Challenge Cup at the Henley Royal Regatta (then considered the equivalent of a World Championship) were present in Rome. Nor did the previous Olympic champions and runners-up, the United States and the Soviet Union, return with their victorious members. The Americans sent " [:a {:href "/athletes/40662"} "Ted Frost"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/40489"} "Robert Rogers"] " of Seattle’s Lake Washington Rowing Club, because they had won the event at the 1959 Pan American Games. The Soviet Union, meanwhile, sent 1959 European runners-up " [:a {:href "/athletes/40220"} "Valentin Boreyko"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/40249"} "Oleg Golovanov"] ". The 1956 Olympic bronze medalists from Austria, " [:a {:href "/athletes/35629"} "Alfred Sageder"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/35597"} "Josef Kloimstein"] ", returned in another bid to capture gold. They were also the most recent European bronze medalists. Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/36193"} "Lorne Loomer"] ", a 1956 Olympic champion in the coxless fours, was replaced by " [:a {:href "/athletes/36071"} "David Anderson"] " in the coxed eights crew that would go on to win silver in that event, and sent to assist " [:a {:href "/athletes/36123"} "Keith Donald"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Of these nations, however, only the Soviet Union managed to win a heat in an opening round that saw strong performances from Germany, Italy, and Finland. The Americans and the Austrians survived to the semi-finals after the repêchage, however, with the latter winning a heat shared with the two global superpowers. The USSR regained its dominance in the final and won a convincing victory over the Austrians, who duked it out for silver with the Finns and emerged victorious by just over a tenth of a second, leaving Finland to settle for bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/40220"} "Valentin Boreyko"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/40249"} "Oleg Golovanov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/35629"} "Alfred Sageder"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/35597"} "Josef Kloimstein"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUT.png"}] "AUT"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/36790"} "Veli Lehtelä"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/36810"} "Toimi Pitkänen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/37640"} "Jochen Neuling"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/37663"} "Heinz Weigel"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "5"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/40662"} "Ted Frost"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/40849"} "Bob Rogers"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:st